Iris — the drawn companion bundled with Omarchy Iris

This folder holds no artwork. The character is code: `keystone/Iris.js`
computes its band, its glass and its halo every frame, and `pet.json` only
says which renderer to use and how large it prefers to be.

That code is a port of the animated orb on https://maia.id/ — the author's
own site — where it is a Framer code component named `SiriOrb`: a four-pass
WebGL2 pipeline whose wave shader draws four copies of one sine wave, spread
in phase across an RGB spectrum, summed additively and composited into a
circular glass panel with refraction and a rim highlight.

The port carries over the CONSTRUCTION, not the technique. It draws on a QML
Canvas rather than in a fragment shader, so the accumulation is layered
strokes instead of per-pixel, and there is no refraction pass — a 2D canvas
cannot sample what is behind it. What is faithful is the geometry: the four
phase offsets, the cosine-squared flank taper, the gaussian that gathers the
band towards the middle, and the additive sum that turns overlapping crests
white while the tails stay saturated.

Where the port departs deliberately, `keystone/Iris.js` says so at the line
that departs — the frequency, the aberration and the falloff are all measured
over a different width here than in the original, and carrying those three
numbers across unchanged does not reproduce the orb, it strangles it.

See the repository's `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md`.
